# Factory settings. Git tracks this file, and the server runs main's copy. Change a value through a pull request to main.
# Secrets, committee ids and host paths live in factory/.env, which never leaves its host. See .env.example. A key in both files stops the factory.
# GitHub repo the factory works on, as "owner/name".
FACTORY_REPO=btseytlin/road-machiners
# Owner and number of the GitHub Project that holds the kanban. Its Status field needs Triage, Design, Implementation, Testing, Approval and Done.
FACTORY_PROJECT_OWNER=btseytlin
FACTORY_PROJECT_NUMBER=2
# A GitHub call that hits a rate limit waits and runs again, with the wait doubled each time. GitHub asks to wait at least a minute after a secondary rate limit, so the waits of 15, 30 and 60 seconds put the last try 105 seconds after the first.
FACTORY_GITHUB_RETRIES=3
FACTORY_GITHUB_RETRY_BASE_SECONDS=15
# A GitHub call that runs longer than this is killed and fails. gh sets no timeout of its own, so a dead connection would hang the tick forever. A normal call takes about a second and a paginated read a few.
FACTORY_GITHUB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
# Docker image of the agent container, built from factory/docker.
FACTORY_IMAGE=roam-factory:dev
# Model ids. FACTORY_DESIGN_MODEL is the Opus id and FACTORY_BUILD_MODEL the Sonnet id. Issue labels pick one of them per stage, see docs/stages.md, section Model routing.
FACTORY_DESIGN_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
FACTORY_BUILD_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
# List prices in dollars per million tokens, as model=input/output/cacheRead/cacheWrite5m/cacheWrite1h, from https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing. They price an agent run cut off before its result event, from its session transcript. They match the cost Claude Code reports for a finished run. Haiku is here because subagents may run on it.
FACTORY_MODEL_PRICES="claude-opus-5-5=4/20/0.2/5/8 claude-sonnet-5-5=2/10/0.2/2.5/4 claude-haiku-4-5-20251001=1/5/0.1/1.25/2"
# Thumbs-up votes that mark an issue for work, and how old in hours it must be first.
FACTORY_MIN_VOTES=1
FACTORY_MIN_AGE_HOURS=0.01
# Hours an author has to answer the questions of triage or design. After that the tick removes needs-info, and the card goes on with the most sensible reading and writes its assumptions into the design comment. No card waits for a person for longer.
FACTORY_NEEDS_INFO_HOURS=24
# The most ElevenLabs generations one sfx:gen run of an agent may make.
SFX_MAX_GENERATIONS=6
# Minutes between ticks. The tick timer takes it only when infra/deploy/deploy.py runs, so a merge into main alone does not change it.
FACTORY_TICK_MINUTES=1
# Live activity refreshes every 10 seconds. The dashboard marks it stale after three missed reports.
FACTORY_OBSERVATION_HEARTBEAT_MS=10000
# Activity messages are small. Ignore oversized agent output events instead of buffering images or large tool results.
FACTORY_OBSERVATION_MAX_EVENT_BYTES=1048576
# Bound the manager's single dashboard snapshot request so an unavailable endpoint cannot hold its turn.
FACTORY_STATUS_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# Minutes a job of each queue may run before the factory stops it. A limit only catches a hung job, so each is about twice the slowest finished run in the server's job logs of 2026-10-03, and at least 15 minutes.
# Triage took at most 2 minutes. The waste review shares the queue and has no measured run yet.
FACTORY_TRIAGE_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=30
# Design took at most 66 minutes.
FACTORY_DESIGN_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=135
# Implementation took at most 162 minutes. Ad hoc, patch and factory change jobs share the queue.
FACTORY_IMPLEMENT_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=330
# Verify is the agent half of the old testing stage, which took at most 134 minutes with the checks.
FACTORY_VERIFY_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=240
# One checks run took about 25 minutes. Checks that only time out run up to three times.
FACTORY_TEST_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=90
# Branch jobs took at most 8 minutes. Ship and incident jobs have no measured run yet.
FACTORY_BRANCH_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=60
# Highest time limit an agent may give one factory-job command. The full checks took at most 34 minutes from 3 to 7 Oct, with a p90 of 22. Agents gave sims and screenshot scripts 60 to 180 minutes, polled them for hours and hit the implement timeout three times.
FACTORY_JOB_MAX_MINUTES=30
# Minutes Hermes has to route a plain reply to an approval post. A reply left unrouted after that becomes a failure for Hermes's incident watch.
FACTORY_REPLY_ROUTE_MINUTES=15
# Fewest words a patch or redesign text may have. A shorter text names no change, so the router refuses it. A design round that runs on a vague text costs hours.
FACTORY_ROUTE_MIN_WORDS=4
# Days between releases to itch.io.
FACTORY_RELEASE_DAYS=7
# Turns of the release playtest's progression run. 2250 turns are 5 in-game days, so the markov bot plays several of the other bot archetypes among the NPC traffic. The run takes minutes, and the Opus review of its log takes the most time.
FACTORY_PLAYTEST_TURNS=2250
# Plays of one release playtest job, the first included. Each play after the first replays the seed on the agent's fixes of the play before. A job that still has findings to fix at the limit blocks the release until a member decides with factory retry.
FACTORY_PLAYTEST_RUNS=4
# One play with its harness and review took 41 to 57 minutes. Four plays with fixes, a check failure round and the checks come to about 3.5 hours.
FACTORY_PLAYTEST_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=330
# Days between waste reviews. Each names the factory's biggest waste from its ledger, beside the period before, and hands it to Hermes, who tells the committee only what matters.
FACTORY_WASTE_REVIEW_DAYS=1
# Public jobs allowed in any 24 hours: triage, design, implement, patch, verify, the release cut, the release playtest and the candidate. Checks, committee-driven jobs, incident entries, /dev/ rebuilds and the waste review do not count. Hotfix jobs count but run at the cap.
FACTORY_MAX_JOBS_PER_DAY=300
# Public jobs one card may start in any 24 hours. A card that loops through retries and send-backs stops here and leaves the daily cap to other cards. The slot of a job that started the card 24 hours ago frees as the window rolls. Hotfix jobs count but run at the limit.
FACTORY_MAX_JOBS_PER_CARD=10
# Jobs that run at once, per queue. Triage, design, implementation and verify each have their own queue, and patch, ad hoc and factory change jobs run in the implementation queue. Verify runs the testing agents. The test queue runs only the factory's checks, which build the game and play it in a browser. Branch jobs, like approve and ship, always run one at a time.
# Memory sets these counts, not CPUs. The host has 15.5 GB. On 2026-10-06, 4 checks, 2 verify and 4 implement jobs filled it, 4.7 GB went to swap, and the kernel killed a job's Chrome 7 times in 20 minutes.
# The checks also share one GPU. On 2026-10-06 the 50 fps floor failed in none of 18 runs alone, 1 of 9 with 2 checks at once, 2 of 12 with 3 and 9 of 39 with 4, so the floor measured the load. Only the release candidate checks it now.
# Checks slow down in line with how many overlap: 9.4 min alone, 12.5 min with 2 and 22.5 min with 4. So 4 at once finish only 10% more checks an hour than 2, for twice the memory.
# Implementation runs three at a time because most issues wait there and agent jobs spend most of their time waiting on the model. Its jobs share the 3 CPUs of the implement pool with verify. The game tests have no time limits on the factory host, so sharing makes them slower and does not make them fail.
FACTORY_TRIAGE_WORKERS=1
FACTORY_DESIGN_WORKERS=2
FACTORY_IMPLEMENT_WORKERS=3
FACTORY_VERIFY_WORKERS=2
FACTORY_TEST_WORKERS=2
# Reasoning effort of the triage agent, passed to claude --effort. Triage only reads and judges, so low is enough.
FACTORY_TRIAGE_EFFORT=low
# Reasoning effort of the design agent, passed to claude --effort. Design at default effort cost $2.51 a run on Opus, the most of any stage. Check the dashboard's design cost after a week on medium.
FACTORY_DESIGN_EFFORT=medium
# Free disk in GB under which a tick starts no job, and Hermes gets a disk incident. Each running job needs about 0.6 GB for its clone, packages and build, and an image rebuild needs room too.
FACTORY_MIN_FREE_GB=5
# Available memory in GB under which Hermes gets a memory incident. The kernel counts the page cache it gives back as available, so a value under this means jobs swap or the kernel is about to kill a container. One checks run peaks near 2.6 GB, so the incident stays quiet for normal peaks and fires when the checks and the agents together fill the memory.
FACTORY_MIN_AVAILABLE_GB=1
# Days a job log stays. Failures live a day and a release spans FACTORY_RELEASE_DAYS, so this keeps two releases of history.
FACTORY_LOG_DAYS=14
# Days an archived agent transcript stays in $FACTORY_HOME/transcripts. They are kept to analyze what agents did and where they got stuck.
FACTORY_TRANSCRIPT_DAYS=10
# Days a game test cache entry stays unused. The test tool touches an entry each time a test file skips on it, so only entries that no checks run needed for this long go.
FACTORY_TEST_CACHE_DAYS=14
# Shares of the server's CPUs, as fractions that add up to 1 or less. Each pool gets round(share × cores) whole CPUs, at least 1, and its job containers are pinned to them. Jobs of one pool share its CPUs, and a pool never borrows from another, so the factory checks always get theirs.
# Triage, design and branch jobs mostly wait on the model or run short builds, so they share one pool.
FACTORY_CPU_LIGHT=0.125
# Implement, patch, ad hoc, change and verify jobs run targeted tests. The implement and verify workers together set how many run at once, so this pool gets the second most CPUs.
FACTORY_CPU_IMPLEMENT=0.375
# Testing runs the full test suite, the typecheck and a browser playtest. Its tests time out when it gets fewer CPUs than this.
FACTORY_CPU_TEST=0.5
# Workers the game's test runner starts in each container of a pool, passed as TEST_WORKERS. Each worker holds 0.4 to 0.9 GB. Agents run focused tests, so 2 workers keep their peak low. A checks run starts one worker per CPU of its pool, as before.
FACTORY_VITEST_WORKERS_IMPLEMENT=2
FACTORY_VITEST_WORKERS_TEST=4
# itch.io page as "user/game". A release pushes to it with BUTLER_API_KEY from .env. The server deploy and the dashboard also need it, for the site's redirect and links.
ITCH_TARGET=btseytlin/road-machiners
# Error reports from the game's release, dev and candidate builds. Each new error opens an error-report issue that skips votes and starts triage, so the daily cap bounds what fake or flooding reports can cost. A report past it is still stored and counted, and Hermes hears of it.
FACTORY_ERROR_DAILY_ISSUES=5
# Total size of stored report files. A mid-game report is about 2 MB gzipped, so this holds about 1000. A report past it is refused, and Hermes hears of it.
FACTORY_ERROR_DISK_MB=2000
# Days the source maps of a dev or candidate build stay. A tab left open on an old build still sends reports for a while. Release maps never go, since players keep old itch tabs.
FACTORY_ERROR_MAP_DAYS=14
# Largest gzipped report. A mid-game report is about 2 MB: two 0.6 MB saves and a 1 MB physics drive. This leaves room for saves 3 times larger.
FACTORY_ERROR_BODY_KB=8192
# Largest report after gunzip, so a gzip bomb stops here. A mid-game report unzips to about 10 MB.
FACTORY_ERROR_UNZIPPED_MB=64
# Reports one address may send per hour. The game sends each distinct error once per session, so a real player sends a few.
FACTORY_ERROR_IP_PER_HOUR=20
# Page origins that may post reports, besides the public URL's own. itch.io serves HTML games from these hosts.
FACTORY_ERROR_ORIGINS="https://html-classic.itch.zone https://html.itch.zone"
